<ArticleLayout title="Apple Watch Sleep & Stress Tracking with Cortisol+" description="Explore available Apple Watch sleep data alongside HRV and recovery in Cortisol+. Understand permissions, missing nights and the limits of a wellness score." published="2026-05-23" updated="2026-09-09" image="/screenshots/sleep-analysis.png">
<p><strong>Cortisol+ brings available sleep data into the same view as wearable stress and recovery signals.</strong> It helps you review your nights and routines. It does not measure next-morning cortisol, identify the cause of a wake-up or determine which sleep stage changed your hormones.</p>
<h2>What sleep data comes from Apple Watch?</h2>
<p>Apple Health can display estimated time asleep and periods classified as awake, REM, core and deep sleep. Availability depends on your watch, software, settings and wear time. Apple's <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108906">sleep tracking guide</a> explains setup and how to inspect sleep history. These are wearable estimates, not a clinical sleep study.</p>
<h2>How sleep contributes to Cortisol+</h2>
<p>The app reads permitted sleep records and uses a sleep-derived factor in its wellness model. Sleep has a 20% base weight before missing-data adjustments and other model modifiers. That weight is a software design choice; it does not mean sleep accounts for 20% of your cortisol production. Read the complete <a href="/how-it-works/">scoring methodology and limitations</a>.</p>
<p>A change in the overall score can reflect several inputs, their freshness and the model's assumptions. Comparing two nights cannot isolate the effect of alcohol, a supplement or a particular sleep stage. We do not promise that a personal hormone pattern will become visible after two or four weeks.</p>
<h2>Getting started with sleep tracking</h2>
<ol><li>Set up sleep tracking and wear a charged, comfortably fitted watch to bed, following Apple's instructions.</li><li>Check that the night appears in Health before looking for it in Cortisol+.</li><li>Review the app's Health permissions and choose which available records to share.</li><li>Note the date of the sleep record and the timestamps of other readings; they may cover different periods.</li></ol>
<p>If sleep or HRV is missing, begin with the source data and permissions. Our <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">missing-data troubleshooting guide</a> explains that decision path for HRV. Enabling read access does not create a measurement that the watch did not record.</p>
<h2>Make an observation journal</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">seven-day stress and sleep journal</a> to record bedtime, wake time, routines and how you felt. Compare those observations with the available records. You can print it or download a blank CSV; the page does not send your entries to analytics.</p>
<h2>Can the app explain a 3 AM wake-up?</h2>
<p>No. A wake-up time and a wearable score cannot establish its cause. Cortisol+ does not diagnose insomnia, sleep apnea, perimenopause or a cortisol disorder. If sleep problems persist or affect your daily life, discuss them with a healthcare professional instead of relying on the app to choose treatment.</p>
<h2>Does a sleep score replace a cortisol test?</h2>
<p>No. Medical cortisol testing uses blood, urine or saliva and is interpreted in the context of the test's timing and purpose. <a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">MedlinePlus explains cortisol tests</a>. Cortisol+'s score is a separate wellness interpretation of wearable data.</p>
<p>See <a href="/features/hrv-stress-monitoring/">HRV stress monitoring</a>, our <a href="/stress/apple-watch/">Apple Watch stress guide</a>, and <a href="/download/">download and subscription information</a> before deciding whether the app fits your needs.</p>
